        class="prose prose-slate max-w-none prose-headings:font-semibold prose-headings:tracking-tight prose-a:text-brand-600"
      >
        <p>
          Lien waivers don't come with much explanation the first time you sign one. Your GC sends a
          form with the check, you sign it, and the job moves on. But a lien waiver is a legal
          document that gives up a property right — and what kind you sign, and when, determines
          whether you're protected or exposed if payment doesn't clear.
        </p>
        <p>
          This guide explains how lien waivers work rather than handing you a form: what the
          conditional vs. unconditional distinction actually means, how progress and final waivers
          differ, where each one lands in the pay application and closeout cycle, and the wording
          that should stop you before you sign. If you already know which one you need, the forms
          are separate pages — a
          <a href="/templates/conditional-lien-waiver/">conditional lien waiver form</a> to send with
          your billing, an
          <a href="/templates/unconditional-lien-waiver/">unconditional lien waiver form</a> for after
          the money clears, or the
          <a href="/templates/lien-waiver/">set of all four lien waiver forms</a>.
        </p>

        <div class="rounded-xl bg-slate-50 border border-slate-100 p-6 not-prose my-8">
          <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900 mb-3">Key takeaways</h2>
          <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm text-slate-600 list-disc pl-5">
            <li>
              There are four standard types: conditional progress, unconditional progress,
              conditional final, and unconditional final.
            </li>
            <li>
              Conditional waivers only take effect when payment clears — safe to sign concurrent
              with payment.
            </li>
            <li>
              Unconditional waivers take effect immediately upon signing — never sign one until
              funds are confirmed in your account.
            </li>
            <li>
              Progress waivers cover a specific billing period; final waivers cover the full
              contract including retainage.
            </li>
            <li>
              Several states mandate statutory forms — using a non-compliant form can make the
              waiver invalid.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="not-prose rounded-2xl border border-brand-100 bg-brand-50 p-6 my-8">
          <p class="font-semibold text-slate-900">Need the forms?</p>
          <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-slate-600">
            Our free <a
              href="/templates/lien-waiver/"
              class="text-brand-600 underline underline-offset-2 font-medium">lien waiver forms</a
            >
            cover all four types — conditional and unconditional, progress and final — as a fillable PDF
            and a Word document, with the retention and pending-change-order exceptions built in. The
            two halves are also on their own pages: the
            <a
              href="/templates/conditional-lien-waiver/"
              class="text-brand-600 underline underline-offset-2 font-medium"
              >conditional waiver forms</a
            >
